---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/ctf.css';
import Cmd from '../../components/Cmd.astro';
import {
  allTermux,
  termuxCount,
  termuxTotal,
  termuxVersion,
  installCommands,
  ctfRepo,
} from '../../lib/termux-ctf';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') + '/';
const list = allTermux();
const count = termuxCount();
const total = termuxTotal();
const version = termuxVersion();
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Termux CTF"
  description={`A capture-the-flag you play in Termux, on your own phone. ${count} challenges, ${total} points, real files on your device.`}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="ctf-hero">
      <h1>Termux CTF</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {count} challenges you play in your own terminal, against files that are really on
        your phone. Real permissions, real tools, no simulator. Every flag is generated
        on your device, so a walkthrough you find online contains someone else's answer.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="ctf-banner">
      <pre>█░░ █▀▀ ▄▀█ █▀█ █▀▄▀█ █░█ ▀▄▀
█▄▄ ██▄ █▀█ █▀▄ █░▀░█ █▄█ █░█
▀▀▀ ▀▀▀ ▀░▀ ▀░▀ ▀░░░▀ ▀░▀ ▀░▀</pre>
      <div class="ctf-banner__note">  Termux CTF   real flags, on your own device

  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    01   hidden-file          Easy     10 pts  open
    02   owner-only           Easy     15 pts  open
    03   two-halves           Medium   20 pts  open
    04   read-the-log         Medium   20 pts  open
    05   twice-over           Hard     30 pts  open
    06   inside-the-archive   Medium   25 pts  open
  ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    0 of 6 captured, 0 of 120 points</div>
    </div>

    <section>
      <h2>Install it</h2>
      <p>
        Termux from F-Droid, then four commands. The installer checks for Termux, prints
        exactly what it is going to write, and waits for you to say yes. Nothing is
        written before that, and nothing needs root.
      </p>
      {installCommands.map((c) => <Cmd cmd={c} />)}
      <p class="ctf-empty">
        Then run <code>ctf list</code>. Run <code>ctf</code> on its own for a menu, which is
        easier than typing on a phone.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn" href={ctfRepo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the source on GitHub</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What makes it different</h2>
      <div class="ctf-cards">
        <div class="ctf-card">
          <span class="ctf-card__mark"></span>
          <h3>The files are real</h3>
          <p>
            A challenge writes real files into your home directory with real permissions.
            You find them with <code>ls</code>, <code>grep</code>, <code>tar</code> and
            <code>base64</code>, the same tools you would use anywhere.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ctf-card">
          <span class="ctf-card__mark"></span>
          <h3>Your flags are yours</h3>
          <p>
            Every flag comes from a random value created on your device at install time,
            and so does every file name. Two people playing the same challenge get
            different answers.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="ctf-card">
          <span class="ctf-card__mark"></span>
          <h3>It works offline</h3>
          <p>
            No account, no server, no network traffic. Once it is installed, the whole
            thing runs on the phone in your pocket.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The loop</h2>
      <p>Every challenge follows the same four steps.</p>
      <ol class="ctf-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Look at what is on offer</strong>
          <span><code>ctf list</code> shows each challenge, its difficulty, and whether you have captured it.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read the briefing and set it up</strong>
          <span><code>ctf start 01</code> teaches the concept, then writes the files into your home directory.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Find the flag</strong>
          <span>This part is up to you. The briefing points at the tool, the hints narrow the search.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hand it in</strong>
          <span><code>ctf check 01 LEARMUX&#123;...&#125;</code> decides. Right or wrong, you know straight away.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        <a class="btn btn--green" href={`${base}ctf/play/`}>How to play, command by command</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The {count} challenges</h2>
      <p>{total} points between them.</p>
      <table class="ctf-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>#</th>
            <th>Challenge</th>
            <th class="ctf-cat">Topic</th>
            <th>Difficulty</th>
            <th>Points</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {list.map((c) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="ctf-id">{c.id}</td>
              <td class="ctf-name">
                <a href={`${base}ctf/challenges/${c.slug}/`}>{c.title}</a>
              </td>
              <td class="ctf-cat">{c.category}</td>
              <td><span class={`chip chip--${c.difficulty.toLowerCase()}`}>{c.difficulty}</span></td>
              <td class="ctf-pts">{c.points}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        <a class="btn" href={`${base}ctf/challenges/`}>Every challenge, with briefings and hints</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Keeping score</h2>
      <p>
        The tool decides whether a flag is right, because it is the only thing that has
        your seed. When you want to see the whole picture here, run
        <code>ctf export</code> and paste the result into the
        <a href={`${base}ctf/progress/`}>progress page</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="ctf-note">
        <strong>Worth being straight about</strong>
        This site cannot check your answers, and it does not pretend to. It has no seed, so
        it has no way to know whether a flag is right, and a receipt you paste in is
        something you told it about yourself. The tool is where the score is real.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Before you start</h2>
      <p>
        Play only on your own device. Everything the tool touches is under your home
        directory, plus one command in <code>$PREFIX/bin</code>. There is no network
        activity anywhere in it, and nothing needs root.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can read the challenge source, because it is on your machine and nothing can
        stop you. The worked solutions are the one thing held back: they live in the
        repository for the test suite, and the installer deliberately does not copy them.
        The score only means something if you earned it.
      </p>
      <p class="ctf-empty">Tool version {version}.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
